# The cooling decision, on its own, with nothing else in the way.
#
# A steam engine with enough to do -- three pumps and two crushers take
# everything it makes -- wedged against a skip so it has no clear air at all,
# and one radiator on the waste port. That radiator is the only cooling in the
# design. There is no jacket, no spacing and no choice of frame: a steam engine
# comes on cast iron and the only other thing it may be built out of is steel,
# which trips at the same 220 the machine itself does.
#
# So the engine settles at 159 degrees, which is HOT, which is 84% of rating.
# It is not a failure and it is not free. That number is the whole of what this
# file is for, because it is a number the model could not produce until the
# duty stopped being five steps: under the bands, anything between 120 and 220
# degrees was a flat 750, so an engine at 121 and an engine at 219 were the
# same machine. They are not.
#
#   20-steamline   engine at  84 degrees   NORMAL   100% of rating
#   22-radiator    engine at 159 degrees   HOT       84% of rating
#
# The difference between those two rows is a jacket and a tile of air.
#
# What the one wire is worth, measured:
#
#   with    `wire SE1.waste -> RD1.heat`    70.69/tick of crushed ore    MET
#   without it                              27.12/tick                   missed
#
# Take it off and the engine trips at 220, latches out until it is back under
# 120, cools, restarts, and trips again -- forever. It spends about half its
# life stopped, the plant makes a third of what it should, and it misses a
# brief it beats by seventy percent with the wire in.
#
# That broken version is not on disk, and the reason is worth writing down
# rather than hiding: it never settles. Twenty thousand ticks and the engine's
# trip cycle has still not come back into phase with the crank and the press,
# so there is no periodic orbit to compile and `machine` can only estimate it.
# Every design in this repository compiles to a transient plus an exact orbit,
# and `tests/machine.rs` holds it to that. A machine that cooks itself is a
# legitimate thing to build and an illegitimate thing to ship.

machine "One Radiator"
brief line

inlet       F1  at 0,0    draws coal
burner      BU1 at 3,0
burner      BU2 at 3,4
pump        P1  at 0,8    draws water
exchanger   HX1 at 7,0
exchanger   HX2 at 7,4
steamengine SE1 at 11,0
skip        SK1 at 11,3
radiator    RD1 at 7,8
gearbox     GB1 at 16,0   ratio 2
crusher     C1  at 19,0
crusher     C2  at 23,0
inlet       I1  at 19,4   draws ore
outlet      O1  at 27,0
mechpump    MP1 at 16,4   frame iron
mechpump    MP2 at 16,7   frame iron
mechpump    MP3 at 11,7   frame iron
skip        SK2 at 19,7

wire F1.out -> BU1.fuel
wire F1.out -> BU2.fuel
wire BU1.heat -> HX1.heat
wire BU2.heat -> HX2.heat
wire P1.water -> HX1.water
wire P1.water -> HX2.water
wire HX1.steam -> SE1.steam
wire HX2.steam -> SE1.steam

wire SE1.rotary -> GB1.in
wire GB1.out -> C1.drive
wire GB1.out -> C2.drive
wire SE1.rotary -> MP1.drive
wire SE1.rotary -> MP2.drive
wire SE1.rotary -> MP3.drive
wire MP1.water -> SK2.liquid
wire MP2.water -> SK2.liquid
wire MP3.water -> SK2.liquid

wire SE1.condensate -> SK1.liquid
wire SE1.exhaust -> SK1.vapour
wire SE1.waste -> RD1.heat

wire I1.out -> C1.in
wire C1.out -> C2.in
wire C2.out -> O1.solid
